@page "/docs/getting-started"
@layout DocsLayout
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Getting Started — AgentBlazor Docs</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-article">
    <section class="docs-hero">
        <p class="docs-hero__eyebrow">Getting Started</p>
        <h1 class="docs-hero__title">From package source to a working route-scoped workflow.</h1>
        <p class="docs-hero__lede">
            The shortest successful path is still one package, one workflow, one route, and one chat surface.
            Start with the CLI if you want the fastest install path for an existing app. Start manually if you want to understand every required runtime hook.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-actions">
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Href="/docs/cli">Use the CLI path</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/demo/workflows/response-orchestration?reset=true">See the live workflow demo</MudButton>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>1. Before you change the app</h2>
                <p>These three checks avoid most setup churn.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-check-grid">
            <div class="docs-check">
                <strong>Pin versions</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Use the same preview version for <code>AgentBlazor</code>, <code>AgentBlazor.Client</code> when applicable, and <code>AgentBlazor.Cli</code>.</li>
                    <li>Do not mix a new CLI with an older runtime package.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-check">
                <strong>Know your host</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Standard Blazor Web App is the easiest automatic path.</li>
                    <li>Hosted WebAssembly uses server-host CLI commands plus browser-safe remote chat in the client project.</li>
                    <li>Legacy/custom hosts can be review-first.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Current preview source:</strong> the public docs path is GitHub Packages. Add the feed and pin the exact version you are validating.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>2. Add the package source and install tools</h2>
                <p>Use the same source and exact version everywhere in the app.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet nuget add source "https://nuget.pkg.github.com/ashpeterson/index.json" \
  --name github-agentblazor \
  --username YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME \
  --password YOUR_GITHUB_PAT \
  --store-password-in-clear-text

dotnet tool install --global AgentBlazor.Cli --version 0.1.0-preview.9 --add-source https://nuget.pkg.github.com/ashpeterson/index.json
dotnet add ./src/MyBlazorApp/MyBlazorApp.csproj package AgentBlazor --version 0.1.0-preview.9 --source github-agentblazor</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>3. Fastest path: CLI-assisted install</h2>
                <p>Use this for an existing app when you want preview-first planning and a written scaffold manifest.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>agentblazor init ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --diff
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --approve
dotnet restore ./MySolution.slnx --force-evaluate
dotnet build ./MySolution.slnx --no-restore -nologo
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>What the CLI still does not do:</strong> it does not generate your provider secrets, choose your production model, or guess through host-specific startup edits that would be brittle.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>4. Manual runtime path</h2>
                <p>This is the cleanest way to understand the AgentBlazor runtime surface area.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>using AgentBlazor;
using MudBlazor.Services;

builder.Services.AddMudServices();

builder.Services.AddAgentBlazor(options =&gt;
{
    options.UseOpenAI(
        apiKey: builder.Configuration["OpenAI:ApiKey"]!,
        model: builder.Configuration["OpenAI:Model"] ?? "gpt-5.4-mini");

    options.ConfigureBuilder(agentBuilder =&gt;
    {
        agentBuilder.AddWorkflow&lt;OperationsCapabilities&gt;(
            "Operations Agent",
            agent =&gt;
            {
                agent.WithDescription("Handles workflow assessment and remediation.");
                agent.WithRoutePrefixes("/ops");
            });
    });
});</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Provider variants:</strong> OpenAI is the most validated provider path today. Azure OpenAI uses the same runtime shape, and Ollama is available when a local OpenAI-compatible endpoint is appropriate.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>5. Add the host shell</h2>
                <p>The exact files vary by host shape. Standard Blazor Web App and hosted WebAssembly are the main automatic paths today.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-callout-grid">
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Standard Blazor Web App</h3>
                <pre class="docs-code"><code>// Components/App.razor
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="@@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.css"]" /&gt;
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="@@Assets[AgentBlazorAssetPaths.Css]" /&gt;
...
&lt;script src="@@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.js"]"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="@@Assets[AgentBlazorAssetPaths.Js]"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

// MainLayout.razor
&lt;MudThemeProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudPopoverProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudDialogProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudSnackbarProvider /&gt;</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Hosted WebAssembly server + client</h3>
                <p>The server host still owns the AgentBlazor runtime. The browser client should use the remote chat components from <code>AgentBlazor.Client</code>,
                not the server-first chat surfaces from <code>AgentBlazor</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Advanced hosts</h3>
                <p>Legacy <code>_Host.cshtml</code> server apps and other recognizable custom hosts stay review-first. The CLI will add safe files,
                then tell you which startup, shell, or layout locations still need manual wiring.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>6. Map endpoints</h2>
                <p>The runtime endpoint must be mapped after the app is built or the chat UI will render without a working backend.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>var app = builder.Build();

app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()
    .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode();
app.MapAgentBlazorEndpoints();</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Hosted WebAssembly note:</strong> if the browser client is using remote chat surfaces, map <code>app.MapAgentBlazorRemoteChat()</code> on the server as well.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>7. Define one capability class</h2>
                <p>Use attributes for semantic actions and let the runtime handle approvals, structured results, and next-step suggestions.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>using AgentBlazor.App;
using AgentBlazor.Attributes;

[AgentCapability("operations",
    Name = "Operations Workflow",
    Description = "Assess workflow readiness and prepare remediation.",
    Category = "Workflow")]
public sealed class OperationsCapabilities(IOperationsService ops)
{
    [AgentAction("Assess current workflow readiness")]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; AssessReadinessAsync()
    {
        var summary = ops.GetReadinessSummary();
        return Task.FromResult(CapabilityResult.Success(summary) with
        {
            NextActions = ["Explain blockers", "Prepare remediation"]
        });
    }

    [AgentAction("Prepare remediation draft", RequiresApproval = true)]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; PrepareRemediationAsync(
        [AgentParam("Target area")] string area)
    {
        var draft = ops.PrepareRemediation(area);
        return Task.FromResult(CapabilityResult.Success(draft));
    }
}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>8. Render a chat surface</h2>
                <p>Use the floating widget for ambient help or the full surface when chat should be part of the page layout.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>@@using AgentBlazor.Components

&lt;AgentChatWidget
    Title="Operations"
    Placeholder="Ask the agent to assess, prepare, or advance the workflow..."
    Width="30rem"
    Height="68vh" /&gt;</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Use <code>AgentChatSurface</code> instead</strong> when you want the chat to stay embedded inside a workflow page or dashboard layout.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>9. Hosted WebAssembly client path</h2>
                <p>Use this when the app has a separate browser client project.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet add ./src/MyBlazorApp.Client/MyBlazorApp.Client.csproj package AgentBlazor.Client --version 0.1.0-preview.9 --source github-agentblazor

// Client Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped(_ => new HttpClient
{
    BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress)
});

// Client _Imports.razor
@@using AgentBlazor.Client.Chat

// Client page or layout
&lt;AgentRemoteChatWidget Endpoint="/agentblazor/chat/run" Title="Assistant" /&gt;</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Remote client rule:</strong> use <code>AgentRemoteChatWidget</code>, <code>AgentRemoteChatSurface</code>, <code>AgentRemoteChatPanel</code>,
            or <code>AgentRemoteChatBar</code> in the WebAssembly client. They call the server runtime over HTTP.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>10. Verify the install</h2>
                <p>Use the runtime and CLI checks together, then run prompts that match the workflow.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet run
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:5288/</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-inline-list">
            <span>Assess the workflow</span>
            <span>Explain blockers</span>
            <span>Prepare the draft</span>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-note">
            Next: use <MudLink Href="/docs/verification">Verification</MudLink> for fresh-app and real-project test flows, then use the
            <MudLink Href="/demo/components">component explorer</MudLink> when you want deterministic wrappers beyond the first workflow.
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
